"""ARCH-40 — Settings Modernization & Unified Review Hub. One atomic, idempotent apply.

Run from backend/:

    python apply_arch40.py --check      # report every file, write nothing
    python apply_arch40.py              # apply
    python apply_arch40.py              # again: every line reads "already applied"
    python apply_arch40.py --rollback   # restore what the last apply replaced
    python apply_arch40.py --list       # the files this script owns

ARCH40-S1:apply. GENERATED from the real diff by gen_apply_arch40.py, which
refuses to emit if any modified file carries no ARCH-40 sentinel.

WHY THIS ENGINE GUARDS ON HASHES, NOT SENTINELS
===============================================

Earlier engines treat "the sentinel is present" as "already applied". ARCH-40
shipped a backend tranche first, and every tranche-1 file already carries an
ARCH40-S1 sentinel — so a sentinel check would silently skip the tranche-2
changes to exactly those files. Each operation here names:

  final_sha    the sha256 of the ARCH-40 result     -> "already applied"
  accepted     the sha256 of every legitimate BEFORE state:
                 the ARCH 38 DONE baseline (8aff568), and
                 the ARCH-40 tranche-1 file, where it differs
                                                     -> replaced
  anything else                                      -> REFUSED, nothing written

Hashes are taken over normalised text (BOM stripped, CRLF -> LF), so a
Windows checkout hashes the same as a Linux one. Writes preserve each file's
own newline style and BOM.

Everything is validated before anything is written. Each write goes to a
temporary file and is renamed into place; a failure restores every file this
run already wrote. The originals are also kept under
backend/.arch40_apply_backup/<timestamp>/ for --rollback.

67 files: 21 new, 46 replaced.
"""

